{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The proposed relation is a reduction claiming SRC_014's two-level warrant validity reduces to two successive standard grounded computations. But the stated refutation condition is too weak to secure reduction: it only filters graphs where an outer-only accepted argument attacks a warrant. A graph can evade that filter yet still refute reduction—e.g., where an argument is grounded-rejected in the inner pass but becomes accepted in the outer pass due to a warrant-level defense, and that same argument attacks an inner argument whose status the outer pass depends on. Since the inner pass is computed first and frozen, it cannot see the warrant-mediated reinstatement that the outer pass introduces, so the sequential grounded computations diverge from a correct two-level semantics. By limiting the guard to 'accepted only in the outer labelling attacks a warrant' (a narrow self-reference case), the target omits the broader circularity where warrant validity and argument labelling are mutually dependent; thus the reduction relation is not adequately substantiated and the content fails the stated problem.","counterexample":["Inner AF: arguments A, B, C with attacks A->B and B->C. Warrant layer: W_B defends B against A's attack, and W_C attacks W_B. In a single-level grounded computation with W_B valid, B reinstates C and C is IN; with W_B defeated, C is OUT. In sequential reduction, the inner grounded pass is computed without warrant mediation: A defeats B, so B is OUT and C is OUT (frozen). Then the outer pass evaluates warrants: if W_C is grounded-IN and attacks W_B, W_B is defeated; but B was already frozen OUT in the inner pass, so C remains OUT even though A (B's attacker) should now be defeated because its only successful attack relied on W_B. A mutually recursive two-level semantics would reinstate B and then C, yielding a different labelling than the sequential reduction."]"}]}